<article>
    <h1>{$selectedSection}</h1>
    <section>
        <h1>Rate of change</h1>
        <p>In physics, physical laws relate one quantity on another. When two quantities depend on each other, an important task is to determine the rate of change of one quantity with respect to the other. For example, we can say that if {`\\(x\\)`} changes by 2, {`\\(y\\)`} changes by 3. In this case, the rate of change of {`\\(y\\)`} with respect to {`\\(x\\)`} is {`\\(\\frac{3}{2}\\)`}. In general, change in {`\\(x\\)`} is denoted by {`\\(\\Delta x\\)`} and change in {`\\(y\\)`} by {`\\(\\Delta y\\)`}. This provides the rate of change as {`\\[\\text{rate of change} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}\\]`} </p>
        <p>Consider the following table</p>
        <table style="border: 1px solid; padding: 0.3em 0.5em; margin:auto;">
            <tr>
                <td>{`\\(x\\)`} </td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>2</td>
                <td>3</td>
                <td>4</td>
                <td>5</td>
                <td>6</td>
                <td>7</td>
                <td>8</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>{`\\(y\\)`} </td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>2</td>
                <td>3.5</td>
                <td>3</td>
                <td>5.4</td>
                <td>4</td>
                <td>7.5</td>
                <td>8.1</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>{`\\(\\Delta x\\)`} </td>
                <td>-</td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>1</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>{`\\(\\Delta y\\)`} </td>
                <td>-</td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>1.5</td>
                <td>-0.5</td>
                <td>2.4</td>
                <td>-1.4</td>
                <td>3.5</td>
                <td>0.6</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        <p>The table shows {`\\(\\Delta x\\)`} for consecutive values of {`\\(x\\)`}. If we consider a larger interval, say, {`\\(\\Delta x = 3\\)`} from {`\\(x = 3\\)`}, then {`\\(\\Delta y = 4 - 3.5 = 0.5\\)`}. This would mean the rate of change to be {`\\(\\frac{0.5}{3}\\approx 0.17\\)`}. But in this interval, the rate of change has many different values like -0.5, 2.4 and -1.4.</p>
        <p>
            The above example shows that a better understanding of rate of change happens when we choose smaller intervals. This becomes difficult when we deal with continuous functions. <em>Differential calculus</em> is about understanding rate of change of continuous functions.
        </p>
        <p>
            The figure below shows an arbitrary continuous function {`\\(y = f(x)\\)`}. In essence, it shows how {`\\(y\\)`} depends on {`\\(x\\)`}. But it does not give us the rate of change immediately. We need to do significant work to know the rate of change of {`\\(y\\)`} with respect to {`\\(x\\)`}. In general, the rate of change is itself a <em>function</em> with variable {`\\(x\\)`}.
        </p>
        <p>Our task is to determine the rate of change of {`\\(y\\)`} at any value of input {`\\(x\\)`}. The idea is to use another value of input {`\\(x + \\Delta x\\)`} for small {`\\(\\Delta x\\)`}. Evalute {`\\(f(x + \\Delta x)\\)`} to get {`\\(y + \\Delta y\\)`}. This will give the rate of change as {`\\[
            \\begin{eqnarray}
            \\text{rate of change} &=& \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}\\\\
                                   &=& \\frac{y + \\Delta y - y}{\\Delta x}\\\\
                                   &=& \\frac{f(x + \\Delta x) - f(x)}{\\Delta x}\\\\
            \\end{eqnarray}
            \\]`} </p>
        <p>If you look at the figure below, then pythagorous theorem would imply that {`\\[
            \\begin{eqnarray}
            \\tan\\theta &=& \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}\\\\
                                   &=& \\frac{f(x + \\Delta x) - f(x)}{\\Delta x}\\\\
            \\end{eqnarray}
            \\]`}</p>
