        </div>
        <p>As we have already concluded, our accuracy of rate of change improves when {`\\(\\Delta x\\)`} is smaller. But how small can we make {`\\(\\Delta x\\)`}? The idea is to approach zero, but never equal to zero (at {`\\(\\Delta x = 0\\)`}, the rate becomes undefined due to division by zero). This limit to making {`\\(\\Delta x\\)`} to zero is written as {`\\[\\lim_{\\Delta x\\to 0}\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{dy}{dx}\\]`} At this limit, the line AB in the graph becomes tangent to the curve at A as point B almost overlaps with A. </p>
        <p>
            {`\\(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\)`} is called the <em>instantaneous</em> rate of change.
        </p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Calculating derivative of a function</h2>
        <p>For a function given as {`\\(y = f(x)\\)`}, its derivative is given by {`\\[\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0}\\frac{f(x + \\Delta x)-f(x)}{\\Delta x}\\]`} How do we use this to calculate the derivative of the function, for example {`\\(y = x^2\\)`}? We are going to see exactly that.</p>
        {`
            \\begin{eqnarray}
            \\frac{dy}{dx} &=& \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0}\\frac{(x + \\Delta x)^2-x^2}{\\Delta x}\\\\
                        &=& \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0}\\frac{2x\\Delta x + (\\Delta x)^2}{\\Delta x}\\\\
                        &=& \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0}\\frac{(2x + \\Delta x)\\Delta x}{\\Delta x}\\\\
                        &=& \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} 2x + \\Delta x\\\\
                        &=& 2x
            \\end{eqnarray}
        `}
        <p>Therefore, we write {`\\[\\frac{dx^2}{dx}=2x\\]`} In general, for any number {`\\(n\\)`} (not necessarily an integer) {`\\[\\frac{dx^n}{dx}=nx^{n-1}\\]`} In your mathematics class, you'll practice computing derivatives more, for now, use the table below </p>
        <table style="margin:auto;">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>{`\\(y\\)`} </th>
                    <th>{`\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\)`} </th>
                    <th>{`\\(y\\)`} </th>
                    <th>{`\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\)`} </th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>{`\\(x^n\\)`} </td>
                    <td>{`\\(nx^{n-1}\\)`} </td>
                    <td>{`\\(\\sec x\\)`} </td>
                    <td>{`\\(\\sec x\\tan x\\)`} </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>{`\\(\\sin x\\)`} </td>
                    <td>{`\\(\\cos x\\)`} </td>
                    <td>{`\\(\\text{cosec}~ x\\)`} </td>
                    <td>{`\\(-\\text{cosec}~ x\\cot x\\)`} </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>{`\\(\\cos x\\)`} </td>
                    <td>{`\\(-\\sin x\\)`} </td>
                    <td>{`\\(\\ln x\\)`} </td>
                    <td>{`\\(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\)`} </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>{`\\(\\tan x\\)`} </td>
                    <td>{`\\(\\sec^2 x\\)`} </td>
                    <td>{`\\(e^x\\)`} </td>
                    <td>{`\\(e^x\\)`} </td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>What about other functions? Most functions of interest in physics are a combination of above functions. Luckily, there are some handy rules of derivatives that can be used to cover a wide variety of functions. We list the rules below. Do not worry, you'll get plenty of time to derive and understand them in mathematics class on derivatives. </p>
        <ol>
            <li>When a function is multiplied by a constant {`\\(c\\)`}, {`\\[\\frac{d}{dx}(cy)=c\\frac{dy}{dx}\\]`} </li>
            <li>
                When a function is sum of two functions  {`\\(f\\)`} and {`\\(g\\)`}, {`\\[\\frac{d}{dx}(f + g)=\\frac{df}{dx} + \\frac{dg}{dx}\\]`}
            </li>
            <li>
                When a function is sum of two functions  {`\\(f\\)`} and {`\\(g\\)`}, {`\\[\\frac{d}{dx}(fg)=g\\frac{df}{dx} + f\\frac{dg}{dx}\\]`}
            </li>
            <li>
                When a function is division of two functions  {`\\(f\\)`} and {`\\(g\\)`}, {`\\[\\frac{d}{dx}\\Big(\\frac{f}{g}\\Big)=\\frac{g\\dfrac{df}{dx} - f\\dfrac{dg}{dx}}{g^2}\\]`}
            </li>
            <li>
                Changing variable of derivative mid-way {`\\[\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy}{dz}\\cdot\\frac{dz}{dx}\\]`}
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p>The table above and the list of these rules, we'll all you need to cover most problems in this physics course.</p>
    </section>
